1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,960
 The majority of engineering ceramics, technical and non-technical are made in the same way.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:09,040
 Ceramic powder is consolidated under pressure by one means or another,

3
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:14,000
 and then is scented or vitrified at high temperatures to allow the particles to kind of

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,840
 bond together. In many ways, the production of these ceramics closely resembles the

5
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,600
 powder metallurgy technique we met for metals all the way back in week four,

6
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:25,760
 and many of the considerations for the design of powder metallurgy components also apply

7
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:29,600
 to the design of ceramic components. In this video we're going to talk about the different kinds

8
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,920
 of consolidation processes for ceramic powders in the different ways that ceramics are

9
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:38,560
 fired and scented. There are quite a few different ways that ceramic powders are consolidated

10
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:43,440
 into a green body or a green compact before they're fired or scented. Perhaps the closest

11
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:48,560
 in spirit to the kind that we met when we talked about powder metallurgy is uniaxial compression.

12
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:52,640
 Here, a ceramic powder is loaded into a diet, negative in the geometry that you want to form,

13
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:57,920
 and a ram is brought down with the other side of that geometry to consolidate the powder under pressure.

14
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:02,480
 This is by and large one of the more common processes because you can achieve reasonably complicated

15
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:07,280
 geometries and get reasonably high densification of the product. And it's also quite efficient

16
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:12,240
 because the same motion that loads the diet can eject the previously pressed part. Of course,

17
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:15,360
 this isn't the only method of consolidation. There are a bunch of different ones,

18
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:20,240
 but another very common one is called isostatic compression. Here, the ceramic powder is loaded

19
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:24,560
 into a flexible mold, and that mold is subjected to hydrostatic pressure from a fluid.

20
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:28,160
 This is very commonly used when you want to achieve a very, very high density product. You

21
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:32,080
 really want to get all the pores you can out of there because you can apply pressure from

22
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:36,080
 all of the different directions. It also helps alleviate some of the considerations you have to

23
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:40,000
 make if you were uniaxially pressing your component. Remember when we talked about powder metallurgy,

24
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,640
 when you're uniaxially compressing your powder, you can't have sudden changes in the geometry of

25
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:49,920
 a component in the direction of pressing because post-press but pre-centred or fired green bodies

26
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:54,640
 or green compats are usually quite brittle and only would otherwise be held together by

27
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:58,880
 electrostatic forces. Surrey powders are normally included with a binder that helps

28
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:03,760
 hold all these things together until they're sintered or until they're fired. These binders are very

29
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:08,480
 often polymers of one form or another in design to burn out or incinerate out as part of the

30
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:13,120
 sintering process. Well, of course, you can consolidate Surrey powders manually. Very often these

31
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:18,080
 processes are highly automated. Just like in powder metallurgy, you normally have a single motion

32
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:22,480
 that deposits some powder in the die while pushing away the previous part. So you've

33
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:27,440
 consolidated your ceramic powder and you formed your green body and now it's time to sinter or fire.

34
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:31,200
 You remember from last week that the primary difference between the manufacturer of technical

35
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:36,000
 ceramics and the manufacturer of fired non-technical ceramics was that in the case of the former,

36
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,400
 our goal is to sinter the particles. To get the particles to meld together at their interface

37
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:45,200
 and achieve densification largely by joining all of the particles together. Whereas in the case

38
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:49,680
 of the latter in vitrification, we do fully sinter some particles and we do partially sinter some

39
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:55,360
 others, but there are also many, many particles that end up unsintered. We instead densify the product.

40
00:02:55,360 --> 00:03:00,640
 We instead join all the particles together by melting a glassy phase in between them, primarily

41
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:05,120
 made of silica and other contaminants. This makes densification of technical ceramics by sintering

42
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:09,760
 generally quite a bit harder than densification of non-technical ceramics by vitrification because

43
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:14,560
 the temperatures involved in the former are so much higher than those involved in the latter.

44
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:18,160
 There are of course exceptions to this rule and other things you need to consider, but to give

45
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:23,520
 you a general idea, tungsten carbide ceramics are generally sintered at temperatures in excess of

46
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:30,000
 2,000 degrees C, whereas clay bricks are generally vitrified at temperatures around 800 to a thousand

47
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,400
 degrees C. And as you can know it output together, these temperatures differ quite substantially,

48
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:38,960
 and they differ substantially again from the temperatures that we saw in powder metallurgy.

49
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:42,880
 Now both temperature and the time at which you hold at that temperature are important for

50
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:47,680
 sintering and vitrification, but generally speaking, a rule of thumb for reasonably time

51
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:52,480
 efficient sintering or vitrification of our product is to aim for about 80% of its melting

52
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:56,880
 temperature. And this generally holds true for both metals and ceramics. Iron, for example,

53
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:00,880
 has a melting temperature in the order of 1,500 degrees, and it's generally sintered in the

54
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:05,440
 order of 1,200 degrees, whereas tungsten carbide, which we talked about sintering above 2,000

55
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:11,520
 degrees C, has a melting temperature in the order of 2,900, 3,000 degrees C. Now getting to 2,000

56
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:16,560
 degrees C to sinter some tungsten carbide is absolutely nothing to sneeze at, but for very,

57
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:22,000
 very high melting temperature ceramics, say in the order of 3,500, 4,000 degrees C,

58
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,120
 melting temperature ceramics, achieving 70 to 80% of this to sinter them is just not practical

59
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:31,280
 or possible. For these materials, a variety of strategies are used to allow us to center lower

60
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:35,760
 temperatures. For example, some ceramics are hot pressed, in other words, pressure is applied

61
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:39,680
 while they're at temperature, and this can help work out some of the porosity without needing

62
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:44,640
 extremely high 70 to 80% temperatures, and sometimes other additives are alloyant,

63
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:48,800
 so put into the ceramics powder to help it sinter. As a last resort, and I say this because

64
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:54,240
 energy is quite expensive, some ceramics are sintered for extremely long times to help

65
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:58,560
 work out all of that porosity. The anodes that are used to melt aluminium, for example,

66
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:03,520
 are made of graphite, which has a melting temperature in excess of 3,500 degrees C. These anodes

67
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:07,760
 use a combination of the strategies we talked about before and still need to be sintered for up to

68
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:12,240
 28 days to work out the porosity. And that's probably where I'll leave these discussions.

69
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:15,840
 There's an excellent video on the manufacturer of tungsten carbide cutting incense that I've

70
